'''3.''' ''pl.'' The bones of the body taken collectively; the skeleton; hence, the bodily frame; a body.
{{smaller block|<poem>Night hangs upon mine eyes: my ''bones'' would rest
That have but labour’d to attain this hour.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., v. 5.}}}}
'''4.''' ''pl.'' Mortal remains: the skeleton or bony structure being the most permanent part of a dead body.
{{smaller block|And Moses took the ''bones'' of Joseph with him: for he had straitly sworn the children of Israel, saying, God will surely visit you; and ye shall carry up my ''bones'' away hence with you. {{right|Ex. xiii. 19.}}}}
'''5.''' The internal shell of cuttlefishes of the family ''Sepiidæ'', having the consistency of bone. Generally called ''cuttle-bone'' or ''cuttlefish-bone.''—'''6.''' Something made of bone, or of a substance resembling bone, as ivory, whalebone, etc.
{{smaller block|(''a'') ''pl.'' Dice. [Slang or colloq.]}}
{{smaller block|He felt a little odd when he first rattled the ''bones''.
{{right|''Disraeli'', Young Duke, ii. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''b'') ''pl.'' A name formerly given to the bobbins used in making lace, because made of bone.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The spinsters and the knitters in the sun,
And the free maids, that weave their thread with ''bones''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., ii. 4.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''c'') ''pl.'' Pieces of bone, ivory, or wood, used in pairs, held between the fingers, and rattled together to produce a kind of music, or to keep time to music.}}
{{smaller block|I have a reasonable good ear in music; let us have the tongs and the ''bones''. {{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iv. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|Peter rolling about in the chair like a serenader playing the ''bones''. {{right|''Mayhew.''}}}}
{{smaller block|(''d'') A strip of whalebone used to stiffen stays, etc.}}
'''7.''' ''pl.'' A person who performs with the bones.

